<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Vanraj Chavda</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">8th century</span> · <a href="/centuries/8th-century">8th century</a> · Also known as Vanaraja</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Vanraj Chavda, or Vanaraja, was the founder of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chavda-dynasty">Chavda dynasty</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gujarat">Gujarat</a> in the 8th century. According to Jain chronicles written centuries later, he was raised by a Jain monk after his father's death in battle and founded the city of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/patan-gujarat">Anahilavada</a> (Patan) around 746, which remained the capital of Gujarat for over five centuries. His history is largely legendary, but he is celebrated in Gujarati tradition.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/vanraj-chavda.jpg" alt="Vanraj Chavda" width="320" height="427" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Nabhas Chandra · CC BY 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Vanar%C4%81ja_C%C4%81va%E1%B8%8D%C4%81_and_his_wives_at_Pa%C3%B1c%C4%81sara_P%C4%81r%C5%9Bvan%C4%81tha_Patan.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>The accounts of Vanraj's youth come from later chronicles that do not agree. Kṛishṇabhaṭṭa's Ratnamālā, of about 1230, says his father Jayaśekhara, the Chavda king of Pañcāsara, was killed in battle by a Chaulukya king named Bhūvaḍa, after sending his pregnant wife Rūpasundarī to the forest with her brother Surapāla, and that Vanraj was born there. Historians doubt this tradition: no city called Kalyāna-kaṭaka lay near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kannauj">Kanauj</a> and no king Bhūvaḍa is known.</p><p>Merutuṇga's Prabandhachintāmaṇi tells that the Śvetāmbara Jain monk Śīlaguṇasūri saw the infant in a hammock on a tree whose shade did not move, took it as a sign, and later judged the boy fit for a kingdom rather than the monastery; these details seem to be the Jain writers' own invention. Vanraj grew up with his uncle Surapāla, who had turned outlaw, and is said to have gained his first wealth by ambushing an envoy party from Kanyākubja and seizing 24 lakhs of drammas and 4,000 horses, with which he raised an army.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>Tradition says a shepherd named Aṇahila showed him the site of his capital, where a hare had turned on a dog, on condition that the city bear his name. Merutuṇga gives the length of his life as 109 years and of his reign as sixty, figures that are hardly possible for a king who came to the throne late in life. His coronation mark was placed by Śrīdevī of Kākara, whom he had promised to treat as a sister, and he made the merchant Jāmba his chief minister. He is also credited with founding <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/champaner">Champaner</a>, named after his general <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/champa">Champa</a>, and was succeeded by his son Yogaraja.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Legacy</h2><p>Jain authors show Vanraj, who was not a Jain by birth, taking part in Jain rituals of kingship. According to the Prabandhachintāmaṇi he built the Panchasara Parshvanath Temple at Śīlaguṇasūri's suggestion, with a statue of himself as a worshipper, and a temple to Kaṇṭeśvarī near his palace; neither survives. His life inspired Mahipatram Rupram Nilkanth's Gujarati novel Vanraj Chavdo of 1881 and films of 1923 and 1930.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Vanaraja Chavda” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Jain chronicles of the 13th and 14th centuries tell how <b>Vanaraja</b>, a prince whose father had been killed in battle, was raised under the protection of a Jain monk and founded the city of Anahilavada, modern Patan in northern Gujarat, around 746.</blockquote><a href="/state/chavda-dynasty">Chavda dynasty</a></li></ul></section>
